<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    DEBUG BUILDS ONLY. This file lives in src/debug/ and is merged into no other
    build type, so the release AAB keeps Android's default: cleartext HTTP
    blocked everywhere, with no exception list to audit.

    Why it is needed: since API 28 `usesCleartextTraffic` defaults to false.
    During development the app talks to the Django dev server over plain HTTP on
    the workshop LAN, and without this every API call fails with a connection
    error that reads exactly like "the server is down" — which sends you to look
    at the wrong machine.

    **It allows any host, deliberately, and that is narrower than it sounds.**
    An earlier version listed one IP address, `192.168.1.3`. It broke the moment
    the router handed out a different lease, and the failure looked identical to
    a server that was not running: no error naming the address, just a refused
    connection. A debug build is not a security boundary — it is signed with a
    throwaway key and carries a development server address — so pinning a subnet
    here bought nothing and cost an afternoon of misdiagnosis.

    The property that matters is the one below this comment: **src/debug**. The
    release build never sees this file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
